<p>Uri was different because of how long the cold lasted. NOAA’s Dallas County entry describes overnight lows near or below 20 degrees almost all week. Combined with the statewide power outages, many homes lost heat entirely, and plumbing that normally rides out a short freeze did not. You can look up other events by date on our <a href="/storm-history/">Dallas storm history</a> page.</p>

<h2>How freezing pipes cause water damage</h2>

<p>Water expands as it freezes. In a pipe, the ice plug pushes pressure toward the closed faucet until the pipe wall splits or a fitting gives way. The break often happens on the coldest night, but you usually do not see it until the ice melts and pressurized water starts pouring out. That delay is why so many Dallas homeowners found damage a day or two after Uri, sometimes while they were still staying somewhere else.</p>

<p>Common trouble spots in Dallas-area buildings include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Attic supply lines</strong>, especially over garages, above exterior walls, and in additions where insulation was thin.</li>
<li><strong>Exterior walls</strong> behind kitchen and bathroom sinks, a common setup in brick-veneer homes.</li>
<li><strong>Crawlspace plumbing</strong> in older pier-and-beam houses in neighborhoods such as Lakewood, the M Streets, Oak Cliff and Kessler Park, and Lower Greenville.</li>
<li><strong>Hose bibs and irrigation backflow assemblies</strong>, which sit fully exposed.</li>
<li><strong>Water heaters and lines in garages or attics</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Fire sprinkler lines</strong> in apartments, offices, and warehouses, where one split head can soak several units or suites.</li>
</ul>

<p>Slab-on-grade homes are not immune. A break inside a wall cavity or above a ceiling can run for hours before anyone notices, soaking insulation, drywall, cabinets, and flooring on its way down.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/The-Moment-That-Changes-Your-Claim-Knowing-When-to-Call-a-Public-Adjuster-1024x683.webp" alt="Man holding a clipboard in a living room with ceiling debris on the floor" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Ceiling collapse is a common sign that a pipe broke in the attic above during a freeze.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Ice storms: limbs, roofs, and service lines</h2>

<p>Not every winter claim is a plumbing claim. North Texas ice storms add weight to everything. Live oaks and other trees that hold their leaves collect ice, and limbs crack and land on roofs, carports, fences, and cars. Ice can pull gutters loose and bring down the service drop to the house. When a limb punctures the roof, melting ice and rain get into the attic, so one event can mean roof damage, interior water damage, and debris removal all at once.</p>

<p>Photograph the limb where it fell before the tree crew cuts it up, note what it hit, and keep the invoice. Many policies cover the damage to the structure and include a limited amount for debris and tree removal. If a power line is down, stay away from it and report it to Oncor. If you smell gas, leave and call Atmos Energy from a safe place.</p>

