<tr><td>Slow or repeated leaks</td><td>Often excluded</td><td>Insurer may argue the leak predates the freeze</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tree limbs on the roof from ice</td><td>Often covered as damage to the structure</td><td>Debris and tree removal sub-limits</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Heat and vacancy conditions</h3>

<p>Many property policies restrict freeze coverage when a building is vacant, unoccupied, or under construction unless the owner used reasonable care to maintain heat, or shut off the water and drained the system. That language matters for rental houses between tenants, homes on the market, properties being renovated, and second homes. A grid-wide outage like the one during Uri can raise its own questions about what “reasonable care” meant when heat was not available. Read the exact wording, because the answer can turn on it.</p>

