<h2>What to do when a pipe bursts</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Shut off the water</strong> at the main valve. Find it before winter. If you cannot reach or turn it, Dallas Water Utilities customers can contact the utility about the meter shutoff; suburban residents should call their own city’s water department.</li>
<li><strong>Turn off power</strong> to wet areas if you can do it safely.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph and video everything</strong> before you move or throw anything away: the break, standing water, sagging ceilings, wet floors, and damaged belongings.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the broken section of pipe</strong> if the plumber removes it, and ask for an invoice that describes the cause in plain words, such as “freeze split in attic supply line.”</li>
<li><strong>Start drying right away.</strong> A mitigation company can extract water, set equipment, and record moisture readings that document how far the water traveled.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> and write down the claim number, the date, and every person you speak with.</li>
<li><strong>Do not discard damaged items</strong> until they are photographed and listed, and ask whether the adjuster wants to see them.</li>
<li><strong>Save every receipt</strong>: plumbing, mitigation, hotel, meals above normal, and supplies.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lays out what to gather, room by room.</p>

<h2>Where freeze claims get disputed</h2>

<h3>“The leak was long-term”</h3>

<p>Stains, rot, or old-looking water marks invite the argument that the leak started long before the freeze. The NOAA dates, a plumber’s description of a fresh split, and photos taken right after discovery all help show that the damage followed the cold. If there was an older, unrelated leak in the same area, say so and separate it; mixing the two only muddies the claim.</p>

<h3>Scope that stops at the visible water</h3>

<p>Water follows framing, wicks up drywall, spreads under laminate and wood floors, and runs behind cabinets. An estimate that covers only the room where the ceiling fell may miss damage in the hallway, the closet, or the room below. Moisture mapping and a room-by-room estimate are the best way to capture it. An <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> can show what the first estimate left out.</p>

<h3>Matching flooring and cabinets</h3>

<p>When the same wood or tile runs through several rooms, or when damaged lower cabinets cannot be matched to the uppers, the question becomes how much has to be replaced for a reasonably uniform result. The answer depends on the policy and the facts, and it deserves documentation rather than assumption.</p>

<h3>Depreciation on contents and finishes</h3>

<p>Many policies pay actual cash value first and release depreciation after repairs or replacement. Our guide to <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a> explains how that holdback works and how to recover it.</p>

<h3>Apartments, rentals, and businesses</h3>

<p>Owners of rental and commercial property face extra conditions. A vacant unit or an office closed for a long weekend can go days before anyone notices a break. Commercial policies may include protective safeguard requirements for sprinkler systems, vacancy clauses, and business income coverage that depends on how long the property is out of service. Tenants’ belongings are usually covered by their own renters policies. See <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> for how these losses are typically handled.</p>

<h3>How long the insurer has to respond</h3>

<p>Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542 sets general deadlines. An insurer generally has 15 days after notice of a claim to acknowledge it, begin its investigation, and request information, and then 15 business days after it receives everything it asked for to accept or reject the claim. It may notify you that it needs more time, up to 45 more days. Payment is generally due within 5 business days after the insurer notifies you it will pay. These deadlines are extended by 15 days after a weather-related catastrophe or major natural disaster declared by the commissioner, which can apply after a statewide freeze. Keep a written log so you can track the dates. For questions about lawsuits or deadlines to sue, talk to a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>Getting ready for the next hard freeze</h2>

<ul>
<li>Insulate exposed pipes in attics, garages, and crawlspaces, and close crawlspace vents during hard freezes.</li>
<li>Disconnect hoses and cover hose bibs and irrigation backflow devices.</li>
<li>Open sink cabinets on exterior walls and let faucets drip when temperatures drop well below freezing.</li>
<li>Know where the main shutoff is and keep the tool to turn it nearby.</li>
<li>If you leave town, keep the heat on or shut off and drain the water, and read your policy’s vacancy language.</li>
<li>Take dated photos and video of your home’s interior before winter so you have a record of its condition.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When a freeze claim needs help</h2>

<p>A drip under the kitchen sink is usually simple. A multi-room loss with ceilings down, floors ruined, and the family in a hotel is not. Dallas Public Adjuster, a service of [[company]], helps homeowners and business owners with <a href="/services/freeze-damage-claims/">freeze damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> across Dallas, Dallas County, and surrounding North Texas communities. That work includes documenting hidden water damage, preparing a detailed Xactimate estimate, and presenting the claim to the insurer. If a freeze claim has already been denied or paid short, see our pages on <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied claims</a> and <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claims</a>.</p>

<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Results depend on the policy and the facts, and a Dallas Public Adjuster cannot promise any outcome. If the water came from outside, such as runoff or a rising creek, read our guide to <a href="/flash-flood-damage-insurance-dallas/">flash flood damage and insurance in Dallas</a>, because flood is handled very differently.</p>
